 - **Backfilling a new index onto existing rows**: specstar extracts `indexed_data` at write time and does NOT auto-backfill — rows written before the index group under `None` and under-count in aggregates. To make them countable, register the model as `Schema("vN").step(None, _reindex_only, source_type=Model)` (the `None` step covers rows written before any `Schema`), then an operator runs the **migrate route** `POST /{model}/migrate/execute` (specstar's `MigrateRouteTemplate` — opt-in, registered globally in `make_spec`) to re-extract their `indexed_data`. `rm.migrate` is the only backfill op; **don't hand-roll a reindex loop** (specstar discussions #365/#366). **When the index is FILTERED on rather than aggregated, a missed backfill is not a wrong number — it is missing rows.** `WorkspaceFile.path` is indexed so `ls(prefix=…)` scopes its query (an unscoped listing cost 796ms on a 3000-file workspace and the entity paths call it ~10 times per interaction), so an unmigrated row answers no `path` predicate and the file tree + every entity list read as EMPTY over intact data — flickering across a 3-replica rollout as pods disagree. Hence `/api/readyz`, which 503s until `filestore.prefix_index_ready()` holds and is what the k8s **readinessProbe** points at (liveness deliberately does NOT, or a pod awaiting a backfill crashloops instead of waiting). Deploy order: roll out → new pods stay unready while the old ones serve → `POST /workspace-file/migrate/execute` → new pods go ready on their own.
